WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.580
Attack surface documentation becomes much more valuable when it maps how information and authority move through the system.

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:21.560
Interfaces include public APIs, private APIs, admin panels, webhooks, file upload paths, message queues, service-to-service calls, identity provider integrations, and third-party connections.

3
00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:26.700
Each interface is a place where assumptions can fail or controls can be misapplied.

4
00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:34.580
Data flows describe how information moves between users, services, APIs, stores, and external parties.

5
00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:45.000
A flow should identify the source, destination, data type, sensitivity, authentication context, transport expectations, and any important processing step.

6
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:47.980
This does not need to be a perfect formal model to be useful.

7
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.480
Even a simple table can reveal unreviewed data sharing or unclear ownership.

8
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:59.720
Trust boundaries mark transitions between different levels of control, identity, sensitivity, or exposure.

9
00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:10.940
Examples include internet to application, user to administrator, application to database, service to third party, development to production, and customer tenant to shared platform service.

10
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.600
These boundaries are where security controls and assumptions deserve attention.

11
00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.520
AI can help convert source evidence into interface and flow summaries.

12
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:29.900
It can identify likely endpoints from API specs, summarize service calls from manifests, and compare diagrams with repository or infrastructure data.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:39.280
But reviewers should validate the result because a model may misread directionality, miss conditional paths, or treat an example endpoint as a production interface.

14
00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:46.440
Documenting flows and boundaries supports threat modeling, access review, incident readiness, and privacy assessment.

15
00:01:46.540 --> 00:02:00.460
It helps teams ask practical questions: who can call this API, what data crosses this boundary, what identity is trusted here, where are logs generated, and what would happen if this interface were misconfigured or abused.
